      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="strategy" aria-labelledby="strategy-title">
      <h2 id="strategy-title">Strategic Guide to Character Selection</h2>

      <h3>Early Game Strategy</h3>
      <p>
        Attack multiplier on a strong starting move wins in early encounters. Scorbunny, Sobble, Litten and Torchic top the early
        character rankings because they combine attack multipliers above 1.06 with S-tier or A-tier moves. During your first runs,
        prioritize killing healers and shielders first, as they support enemy packs and extend fights dangerously. Step out of
        the filling circles and lines telegraphed on the ground to avoid taking unnecessary damage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Movement and positioning become crucial even in early stages. Partners with 105+ speed can kite effectively, staying just
        ahead of enemy melee range while dealing damage. This kiting strategy works particularly well with projectile moves like
        Water Gun and Ember that hit at range. Confusion's homing property removes the need for precise aiming, making it easier
        for players new to the genre to land consistent damage.
      </p>

      <h3>Mid Game Boss Strategy</h3>
      <p>
        Five bosses arrive at minutes 3, 6, 9, 12 and 15, each with significantly more health than regular enemies. Bosses and
        legendaries telegraph 4 major attacks with visual indicators, giving you time to dodge. They enrage at half HP, dealing
        increased damage and speeding up their attack patterns. High attack multipliers like Haxorus (×1.51) excel here because
        they can burst through boss health pools quickly, ideally ending fights before enrage mechanics make them dangerous.
      </p>
      <p>
        Boss positioning requires different tactics than swarm clearing. Stand at mid-range to see telegraph indicators clearly
        while maintaining damage uptime. Piercing moves gain extra value against bosses because many spawn with supporting enemies,
        letting your attacks hit both the boss and its reinforcements. Evolution cards become available during mid-game, so plan
        your stat card picks to unlock your move's evolution before the minute 15 boss if possible.
      </p>

      <h3>Late Game Endurance Strategy</h3>
      <p>
        From minute 30 the run transforms into an endurance test. Garchomp and Dragapult lead the late-game rankings because
        they pair high HP (119 and 110 respectively) with exceptional speed (121 and 138) for dodging. The Arceus phase spawns
        legendary Pokémon every 5 seconds with health and damage that doubles each wave, making it mathematically impossible to
        kill everything. Instead, focus on survival through positioning and defensive play.
      </p>
      <p>
        Speed matters more than damage in late-game because you need to dodge constantly while maintaining safe distance from
        exponentially scaling enemies. HP pools provide margin for error when telegraphs overlap or spawn patterns trap you
        temporarily. Partners with both attributes can survive longer, giving you more time to climb leaderboards. Avoid greedy
        damage attempts that put you in danger—in endless mode, staying alive for 10 more seconds beats a single extra kill.
      </p>

      <h3>Held Items and Bonds</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Choice Band:</strong> +40% damage, but you can't switch moves.</li>
        <li><strong>Quick Claw:</strong> −20% cooldown.</li>
        <li><strong>Life Orb:</strong> +30% damage, attacks cost HP.</li>
        <li><strong>Shell Bell:</strong> heal from damage dealt.</li>
        <li><strong>Bonds:</strong> each rift legendary beaten links two moves to fire at once from one slot, up to 4 bonds.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Held items dramatically alter partner performance by modifying core stats or adding new mechanics. Choice Band provides
        the largest damage boost at +40%, but locks you into a single move slot, removing flexibility. This trade-off works best
        on partners with powerful primary moves who don't rely on move switching. Quick Claw's −20% cooldown reduction effectively
        increases DPS by letting you attack more frequently, pairing well with high-damage moves like Ember or Dragon Rage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Life Orb offers +30% damage at the cost of HP drain on each attack, creating a risk-reward dynamic that suits
        high-speed partners who can kite effectively and avoid incoming damage. Shell Bell provides sustain through
        healing based on damage dealt, turning aggressive play into survivability. Legendary bonds represent the most
        powerful mechanic: each rift legendary beaten links two moves to fire simultaneously from one slot, up to 4 bonds.
        This effectively doubles your damage output per slot, making bond accumulation a critical objective in longer runs.
      </p>

      <h3>Co-op Team Composition</h3>
      <p>
        Up to 4 players share XP in multiplayer runs, creating unique strategic considerations. A downed player can be
        revived by a teammate standing next to them for 3 seconds, making tanky partners like Slowpoke or Snorlax valuable
        team picks because they can safely approach downed allies. Piercing moves gain extra utility in co-op because
        enemy packs cluster more densely, letting pierce effects hit multiple targets including healers positioned behind
        front-line enemies. Team composition should balance damage dealers with at least one defensive partner for safe revivals.
      </p>

      <h3>Common Mistakes That End Runs Early</h3>
      <p>
        New players frequently make the same tactical errors that shorten runs or prevent reaching minute 30. The most
        common mistake is ignoring enemy healers and shielders in favor of high-damage targets. Healers restore large
        chunks of enemy HP and multiply effective enemy health by 2-3×, making them priority targets despite lower
        individual threat. Always scan for healers (identified by green healing circles around nearby enemies) and
        prioritize them with piercing moves that bypass front-line protection.
      </p>
      <p>
        Standing still while attacking represents another critical error. PokeSurvivor rewards constant movement—kiting
        in circles or figure-eights while maintaining damage uptime. Partners with 100+ speed excel at this pattern,
        staying just outside melee range while projectile moves hit pursuing enemies. Static play lets enemy packs
        surround you, turning avoidable damage into guaranteed hits that drain HP pools needed for late-game survival.
      </p>
      <p>
        Card selection mistakes compound over time. Taking damage cards exclusively creates glass cannon builds that
        die quickly in the Arceus phase where survivability matters most. Balance offensive cards (Power, Reflexes)
        with defensive options (HP, Regen, Speed). Evolution cards deserve priority because evolved moves provide
        significant DPS increases—for example, Ember evolves into Fire Blast with the Power card, nearly doubling
        damage output. Missing evolution timing by taking the wrong stat card wastes the entire run's potential.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, underestimating the minute 30 difficulty spike catches players unprepared. Arceus fundamentally changes
        the game from an offense-focused clear to a defense-focused survival test. Players who enter this phase with
        low HP pools (under 100) or slow partners (under 100 speed) find themselves unable to dodge telegraphed attacks
        or absorb mistakes. Plan your build progression with late-game requirements in mind from the start, not just
        early clearing speed.
      </p>

      <h3>Understanding Arceus Phase Scaling Math</h3>
      <p>
        At exactly minute 30, Arceus appears and takes players to the Ruins of Judgment, initiating endless mode.
        This phase features exponential scaling that makes traditional clear strategies impossible. Every 5 seconds a
        new legendary Pokémon spawns with exactly double the HP and damage of the previous one. The first legendary
        might have 500 HP; the second has 1,000 HP; the third has 2,000 HP; the tenth has 256,000 HP. This doubling
        continues indefinitely, creating enemies you cannot meaningfully damage after just 10-15 spawn cycles.
      </p>
      <p>
        According to the game's spawn code (ruins.js, commit c31715d), legendary spawn timing follows a fixed 5-second
        interval with no variance. The HP multiplier formula is <code>baseHP × 2^(waveNumber - 1)</code>, and damage
        scales identically. By wave 15 (at approximately minute 31:10), enemies have 16,384× base stats. Even a ×1.51
        attack multiplier partner like Haxorus with fully upgraded moves cannot kill fast enough to prevent screen
        saturation with invulnerable threats.
      </p>
      <p>
        This mathematical reality explains why the late-game character rankings prioritize HP and speed over attack
        multipliers. Damage becomes irrelevant when enemies scale exponentially while your stats scale linearly through
        card bonuses. Garchomp's 119 HP provides buffer for 2-3 hits from scaled legendaries; Dragapult's 138 speed
        creates enough distance to dodge telegraph patterns. Survival time correlates directly with mobility and
        mistake tolerance, not damage output. Leaderboard runs past minute 35 require near-perfect dodging, making
        speed the only stat that still matters.
      </p>

      <h3>Move Speed Analysis: Water Gun vs Thunder Shock</h3>
      <p>
        Water Gun ranks as the fastest starting move at 0.44-second cooldown, giving it best-in-class DPS among the
        six starter options. This explains why Water Gun partners dominate the early S and A tiers despite moderate
        per-hit damage (14). The cooldown advantage means approximately 136 shots per minute compared to Ember's 115
        shots, closing the damage-per-hit gap through volume. Water Gun's pierce property adds additional value by
        hitting 2 enemies per shot, effectively doubling output in dense packs.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, gacha partners introduce Thunder Shock with a 0.40-second cooldown—10% faster than Water Gun and
        the fastest move we have verified in the official moves.js data. Jolteon carries Thunder Shock with ×1.31
        attack and 133 speed, making it a strong mid-game upgrade over any starter. Thunder Shock also pierces 2
        targets like Water Gun, matching its multi-target utility. The 0.04-second cooldown difference translates
        to 150 shots per minute, giving Thunder Shock users 10% more DPS uptime than Water Gun at equivalent attack
        multipliers.
      </p>
      <p>
        This comparison illustrates why move cooldown matters as much as damage per hit. A 0.40s move at 13 damage
        (Thunder Shock: 32.5 DPS) outperforms a 0.52s move at 18 damage (Ember: 34.6 DPS) once attack multipliers
        equalize through partner stats and card bonuses. Faster cooldowns also improve kiting effectiveness because
        shorter animation locks allow more frequent repositioning. When evaluating partners, consider both the move's
        base cooldown and the partner's attack multiplier—the cooldown determines your theoretical max DPS ceiling
        while the multiplier scales that ceiling upward.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="changelog" aria-labelledby="changelog-title">
      <h2 id="changelog-title">Character Ranking Update History</h2>
      <p>
        This guide receives regular updates to match game balance changes and newly discovered strategies. Each update
        verifies partner statistics against the official source code to ensure accuracy. Check this section periodically
        to understand how recent patches have affected character performance and rankings.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {changelog.map((c) => <li><strong>{c.date}:</strong> {c.text}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <FAQ heading="PokeSurvivor tier list FAQ" items={faqItems} />

    <aside class="callout callout-warning">
      <p>
        Fan-made, not official. Game facts come from the official guide, the
        <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/moves/" rel="noopener">official moves page</a> and the
        <a href="https://github.com/JPTER78/pokesurvivor" rel="noopener">public source code</a> (commit c31715d).
        If our pokesurvivor tier list disagrees with the game, trust the game. (Some players search for a "Poké
        Survivor tier list" or "tier list PokeSurvivor"; it is the same game and this is the same list.) Related: <a href="/best-pokemon/">best
        starter picks</a>, <a href="/rifts/">rifts guide</a>, <a href="/co-op/">co-op guide</a>.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
